---
title: Introduction
---
body:

<h1>Introduction</h1>

<p>Euclid&rsquo;s <i>Elements</i> form one of the most beautiful and
influential works of science in the history of humankind. Its beauty
lies in its logical development of geometry and other branches of
mathematics. It has influenced all branches of science but none so
much as mathematics and the exact sciences. The <i>Elements</i> have
been studied 24 centuries in many languages starting, of course, in
the original Greek, then in Arabic, Latin, and many modern languages.

<p>Dr. Joyce created this version of Euclid&rsquo;s <i>Elements</i>
to rekindle an interest in the work, and to show how interactive
diagrams could help bring the <i>Elements</i> alive on the web. The
text of all 13 books is complete, and every figure &mdash; even those
in the last three books on solid geometry that are three-dimensional
&mdash; is illustrated by a live, draggable diagram.

<p>This edition renders the diagrams with
<a href="/geomlib/"><tt>geomlib</tt></a>, a TypeScript port of the
original 1996 Geometry Applet. The diagrams are interactive: drag a
free point and the rest of the construction follows. For details on
how to interact with the figures, see
<a href="/geomlib/usingGeomlib.html">Using geomlib diagrams</a>; for
the full proposition list, head to the
<a href="/elements/">Table of Contents</a>.

<p>Also republished on this site are these related tutorials:

<ul>
  <li><a href="/geomlib/compass/"><b>Compass Geometry</b></a> &mdash;
      a seven-part introduction to Euclidean constructions using
      compass and straightedge.</li>
  <li>Spherical and inversive geometry tutorials:
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/geomlib/round/round1.html"><b>Round triangles</b></a></li>
      <li><a href="/geomlib/round/sixeight.html"><b>Configuration of six circles and eight points</b></a></li>
      <li><a href="/geomlib/round/desargues.html"><b>Desargues' Theorem</b></a></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><a href="/geomlib/eulerline.html"><b>The Euler Line</b></a> &mdash;
      a single-page interactive demo of the Euler line of a triangle.</li>
</ul>

<p style="text-align:center; margin-top:1.5em;">
  <a href="/elements/"><b>Read the Elements &rarr;</b></a>
</p>
